<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Heligoland–Zanzibar Treaty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1890">1890</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Anglo-German Agreement of 1890</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Heligoland–Zanzibar Treaty was signed on 1 July 1890 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Germany recognised a British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> over Zanzibar and Pemba and British interests in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a> and gave up claims in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/witu">Witu</a>, while Britain ceded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> island of Heligoland to Germany. Zanzibar became a British protectorate later that year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/heligoland-zanzibar-treaty.jpg" alt="Heligoland–Zanzibar Treaty" width="320" height="223" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-2005-0151,_%C3%9Cbergang_Helgolands_an_das_Deutsche_Reich.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin-conference">Berlin Conference</a> of 1884, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> had been losing out in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scramble-for-africa">Scramble for Africa</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-east-africa-company">German East Africa Company</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-peters">Carl Peters</a> had acquired a strip of land on the Tanganyikan coast, which led to the Abushiri Revolt of 1888, but it never had any control over the islands of the sultanate of Zanzibar. Meanwhile the construction of the Kiel Canal, begun in 1887, made control of the German Bight essential to Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Wilhelm II</a>'s plans for the Imperial Navy. The islands of Heligoland, which had belonged to the dukes of Holstein-Gottorp and had been a British possession since 1814, lay across those approaches. The treaty served the aim of the German chancellor, Leo von Caprivi, of a settlement with the British.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>Under the agreement of 1 July 1890, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> gained Heligoland, the Caprivi Strip in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a>, which gave German South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a> access to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambezi">Zambezi</a>, and a free hand on the coast around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dar-es-salaam">Dar es Salaam</a>, the core of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-east-africa">German East Africa</a>. In exchange Germany handed over its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> over the small sultanate of Wituland on the Kenyan coast, with parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a> that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> needed for a railway to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-victoria">Lake Victoria</a>, and pledged not to interfere with British actions towards the independent sultanate of Zanzibar, meaning the islands of Unguja and Pemba. The treaty also set the German sphere of interest in South-West Africa and settled the borders between Togoland and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-coast">Gold Coast</a> and between Kamerun and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> gave up a naval base that covered the approaches to the main German bases in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>. It soon declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> over Zanzibar and gained full control of the sultanate in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-zanzibar-war">Anglo-Zanzibar War</a> of 1896. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelm-ii">Wilhelm II</a>'s naval policy later ended hopes of an accommodation with Britain and helped bring Britain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> together in the Entente cordiale of 1904.</p><p>The popular name was introduced by the former chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-von-bismarck">Otto von Bismarck</a>, who wanted to attack his successor Caprivi over an agreement that Bismarck himself had arranged. It implied that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> had swapped an African empire for a tiny island, trousers for a button, and imperialists took it up and complained of treason. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-peters">Carl Peters</a> and Alfred Hugenberg called for what became, in 1891, the Pan-German League. G. K. Chesterton described Heligoland as another fragment of the British robe that Lord Salisbury had cast away almost contemptuously in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>.</p><p>The islanders had only a minor part in the talks, and the British press called for a vote on the island. The treaty kept the existing laws and promised the continuation of local practices: no taxes until 1918, exemption from duty until 1910, and conscription only for those born in 1890 or later.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Heligoland–Zanzibar Treaty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the <b>Heligoland–Zanzibar Treaty</b> of 1 July 1890 Germany recognised British claims, and Zanzibar and Pemba became a British protectorate later that year.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-zanzibar">Sultanate of Zanzibar · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The song was first sung at an official ceremony in 1890, on Heligoland, when the <b>Heligoland–Zanzibar Treaty</b> made the island German, and it became one of the best-known patriotic songs of the German Empire.</blockquote><a href="/songs/deutschlandlied">Deutschlandlied</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the <b>Heligoland-Zanzibar Treaty</b> of 1890 Germany handed Witu to Britain.</blockquote><a href="/state/witu-sultanate">Witu Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
